From c2cf6d978e8660a8e30ad6a7b3ecaba4d411fe77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:24:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the GPU's use and temperature in the player
 information

The overview page of the player information gives the processor's use and
temperature but nothing of the GPU, which does the Dolby Vision work. A user
with a laptop asked for both (LibreELEC Hades issue #1), worried about heat.
A row "GPU" now gives them, after the CPU's, from what the driver of the GPU
that Kodi draws with gives, and from nothing else:

- amdgpu: gpu_busy_percent, and the hwmon channel labelled "edge" (temp1,
  the only one of an APU);
- nvidia: NVML, libnvidia-ml.so.1 of the driver's user space (the image has
  it), loaded when first needed; the GPU by the PCI address of the DRM
  device; nvmlDeviceGetUtilizationRates() and nvmlDeviceGetTemperatureV() of
  the sensor of the die. The few declarations of NVML here were checked
  against the nvml.h of the same release (615.78.08);
- i915 and xe: the share of the time the render engine was busy between two
  readings, from the driver's PMU through perf (i915: rcs0-busy over the time
  the counter was enabled; xe: engine-active-ticks over engine-total-ticks),
  and a temperature only where the driver registers a hwmon device for the
  GPU, which it does for discrete GPUs alone (i915: temp1; xe: "pkg").

The use is that of the graphics engine on each driver, not of the video
decoder. What the driver does not give is not shown: the use alone, "use not
known" with the temperature, or "not known"; a temperature that a GPU at work
cannot have (0 or less, over 150 C) is not one. An integrated Intel GPU has
no temperature of its own.

Reading can wait for the driver (amdgpu asks its SMU firmware; NVML's
initialisation is slow), so it is done by a thread of its own
(GPUSENSORS::CSampler), made when the dialog is first shown. The thread that
draws asks at most every 500 ms while the dialog is visible, as for the rest
of the page, and takes the last whole reading without waiting, for the reading
or for a lock. What is opened for the readings (NVML, the perf counters) is
let go when the dialog closes; on xe, the counter of the render engine holds
the GT awake while it is open. Where the values come from, or why they are not
known, is written to the log once.
